// run
// A generic interface's OWN method resolves on a value held in an
// interface-typed binding (#5276). `Store<i64>` used to have no entry in
// the method tables at all: `copyMethodsToInsts` walks `Tag.FuncDecl`
// receiver methods onto a class's instantiations, but an interface's own
// methods are `Tag.MethodSig` entries on the `InterfaceDecl` itself, never a
// `FuncDecl` — so the sweep found nothing to copy and every instantiation's
// method table stayed empty. `s.get("k")`, called through the
// interface-typed binding `s`, failed with E0057 even though `get` is
// declared right there on `Store`.
//
// The implementer's own type param is spelled `V`, not `T`: the two are a
// SEPARATE bug in `buildSignatures` (checkbind.bit) — filed on #5276 rather
// than fixed here — where a second generic sharing a param NAME with `Store`
// silently overwrites which TypeId that spelling resolves to for the whole
// module, and `Store.get`'s own interned signature ends up mentioning the
// wrong one. This fixture pins only the lookup bug the ticket is about.
interface Store<T> {
  get(key: string): T,
}
class ArrayStore<V> {
  v: V
  get(key: string): V {
    return this.v
  }
}
fn main() {
  let s: Store<i64> = ArrayStore<i64>{ v = 42 }
  print("${s.get("k")}\n")
}
